The '''Peruvian Hairless Dog''' or {{lang|es|'''Perro Sin Pelo del Perú'''|i=no}} is a Peruvian [[list of dog breeds|breed]] of [[hairless dog]]. It is the only living breed of dog indigenous to Peru.<ref name=pe>{{Cite web|title=PERRO PERUANO SIN PELO (Canis familiaris)|url=https://www.peruecologico.com.pe/fau_perrosinpelo_1.htm|website=www.peruecologico.com.pe|access-date=2020-05-14}}</ref>

As in other hairless breeds, coated examples can also occur.<ref name=fci2>{{Cite web|title=FCI Breeds Nomenclature | url = http://www.fci.be/en/nomenclature/ | website=www.fci.be |access-date=2020-05-14}}</ref> In hairless examples the skin may be any one of a wide variety of colors, with or without limited unpigmented areas, usually the legs, tail or chest; any hairs may be of any colour. In coated dogs, the coat may be of any colour but [[merle (dog coat)|merle]].{{r|fci2}}

Hairless dogs are associated with the [[Andean civilizations]]. The modern breed is recognized as a part of the national [[cultural heritage of Peru|cultural heritage]].<ref>{{Cite web|title="Designan representante del Ministerio ante el Comité Nacional de Protección del "perro sin pelo del Perú". Resolución ministerial n.º 076-2013-MC"|url=https://www.mef.gob.pe/contenidos/servicios_web/conectamef/pdf/normas_legales_2012/NL20130313.pdf|last=|first=|date=|website=Ministerio de Economía y Finanzas del Perú (Lima)|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130615132817/http://www.mef.gob.pe/contenidos/servicios_web/conectamef/pdf/normas_legales_2012/NL20130313.pdf|archive-date=2013-06-15}}</ref><ref name="BBC News">{{Cite news |date=2019-09-02 |title=Masters of the pyramid: The dogs reclaiming their heritage |language=en-GB |work=BBC News |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-49471593 |access-date=2023-09-29}}</ref>

== Naming ==

The dog has been known by many names, among them ''Allqu'' (in Inca times), {{langx|qu|Kaclla}}, {{langx|es|Perro calato}}, {{lang|es|Perro chino}} (which may derive from {{lang|qu|china}}, 'woman', rather than indicating a Chinese origin<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Cook |first1=O. F. |title=DOMESTICATION OF ANIMALS IN PERU Get |journal=Journal of Heredity |date=April 1919 |volume=10 |issue=4 |pages=176–181 |doi=10.1093/oxfordjournals.jhered.a101913 |url=https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfordjournals.jhered.a101913 Published: 01 April 1919|url-access=subscription }}</ref>), {{lang|es|Perro de Sechura}}, {{lang|es|Perro Chimú}}, {{lang|es|Perro orquídea}}, {{lang|es|Viringo}}{{r|fci2}} and {{lang|es|Vitilingo}}.{{r|pe}}

== History ==
[[Image:Ancient Dogs Brunning.jpg|thumb|[[Andean ceramics|Huacos]] of ancient Peruvian Hairless Dogs, [[Brüning Museum]].]]
The Peruvian Hairless Dog is often perceived to be an Incan dog because it is known to have been kept during the [[Inca Empire]] (the Spaniards classified them as one of the six different breeds of dogs in the empire), and they were also kept as pets in pre-Inca cultures from the Peruvian northern coastal zone. Ceramic hairless dogs from the [[Chimú]], [[Moche (culture)|Moche]], and [[Vicus (Peru)|Vicus]] culture are well known. Depictions of Peruvian hairless dogs appear around A.D. 750 on [[Moche (culture)|Moche]] ceramic vessels and continue in later [[Andean ceramic]] traditions.<ref>Berrin, Katherine & [[Larco Museum]]. The Spirit of Ancient Peru:Treasures from the Museo Arqueológico Rafael Larco Herrera. New York:Thames and Hudson, 1997.</ref>{{page needed|date=July 2020}}

Peruvian Hairless Dogs are now a symbol of Peru and part of its national heritage, celebrated in art and literature.<ref name="BBC News"/> The [[Fédération Cynologique Internationale]] (FCI) accepted the breed and adopted an official [[breed standard]].{{citation needed|date=February 2024}}

Before that time, in the United States, some enthusiasts created another type of Peruvian hairless dog, the Peruvian Inca Orchid. The Peruvian Inca Orchid is recognized by the AKC and all recognized dogs are descendants of 13 dogs brought from Peru in the early 20th century.{{citation needed|date=February 2024}}

== Characteristics ==
[[File:Peruvian Hairless Dog 1.jpg|thumb|A perfect Peruvian Hairless Dog pedigree. Trujillo, Peru.|300px]]
[[File:Peruvian Hairless Dog.jpg|thumb|Face of a typical Peruvian Hairless Dog.]]
[[File:PeruvianDog.jpg|thumb|Female Peruvian Hairless Dog sitting.]]
According to the [[Fédération Cynologique Internationale|FCI]] [[breed standard]], the most important aspect of its appearance is its hairlessness. Hairless dogs are often used in research for testing of various conditions such as dermatitis and other immune-related conditions.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Kimura |first1=Ohshima |title=The inheritance and breeding results of hairless descendants of Mexican hairless dogs |journal= Laboratory Animals|date=January 1993 |volume=27 |issue=1 |pages=55–58 |doi=10.1258/002367793781082403 |pmid=8437436 }}</ref> The dog may have short hair on top of its head, on its feet, and on the tip of its tail. In Peru, breeders tend to prefer completely hairless dogs. The color of skin can be chocolate-brown, elephant-grey, copper, or mottled. They can be totally one color or one color with tongue pink spots. [[Albinism]] is not accepted. The eye color is linked to the skin color. It is always brown, but dogs with light colors can have clearer eyes than darker-skinned dogs.<ref name="FCI">{{cite web |title=Perro sin pelo du Peru |url=https://www.fci.be/Nomenclature/Standards/310g05-en.pdf |website=Federation Cynologique Internationale |access-date=25 February 2024}}</ref>

Peruvian Hairless Dogs come in three sizes:<ref>{{Cite web |title=Peruvian Inca Orchid - Dog Breed Information |url=https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/peruvian-inca-orchid/ |access-date=2023-09-29 |website=American Kennel Club |language=en}}</ref>
*Small {{convert|25|–|40|cm|in|round=0.5|abbr=on}}
*Medium {{convert|40|–|50|cm|in|round=0.5|abbr=on}}
*Large {{convert|50|–|65|cm|in|round=0.5|abbr=on}}

Weight is also varied according to size :
*Small  4&nbsp;– 8&nbsp;kg (9&nbsp;– 18&nbsp;lbs)
*Medium  8&nbsp;– 12&nbsp;kg (18&nbsp;– 26&nbsp;lbs)
*Large  12&nbsp;– 25&nbsp;kg (26&nbsp;– 55&nbsp;lbs)

The dogs should be slim and elegant, with the impression of force and harmony, without being coarse.<ref name="FCI"/> The ears should be candle-flame shaped and erect with the possibility to lay flat.<ref name="FCI"/> Proportions of height (at [[withers]]) to length (withers to base of tail) are 1:1.<ref name="FCI"/>

[[Image:Panasonic Lumix Cam 008.jpg|thumb|right|Peruvian Hairless Dog, US.]]
The gene that causes hairlessness also results in the dogs often having fewer teeth than other breeds, mostly lacking [[Teeth|molar]]s and premolars.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Kupczik |first1=Kornelius |last2=Cagan |first2=Alexander |last3=Brauer |first3=Silke |last4=Fischer |first4=Martin S. |date=2017-07-14 |title=The dental phenotype of hairless dogs with FOXI3 haploinsufficiency |journal=Scientific Reports |volume=7 |issue=1 |page=5459 |doi=10.1038/s41598-017-05764-5 |issn=2045-2322 |pmc=5511229 |pmid=28710361|bibcode=2017NatSR...7.5459K }}</ref>

The hairlessness trait is an [[Dominance_(genetics)#Incomplete_dominance_(non-Mendelian)|incompletely dominant]] [[lethal allele|lethal mutation]], which means that hairlessness does not breed true. Embryos homozygous for the mutation do not develop in the womb. This results in an average birthrate of 2:1 of hairless to coated puppies.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Parker |first=Heidi G. |last2=Harris |first2=Alexander |last3=Dreger |first3=Dayna L. |last4=Davis |first4=Brian W. |last5=Ostrander |first5=Elaine A. |date=2017-02-05 |title=The bald and the beautiful: hairlessness in domestic dog breeds |url=https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rstb.2015.0488 |journal=Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences |language=en |volume=372 |issue=1713 |article-number=20150488 |doi=10.1098/rstb.2015.0488 |issn=0962-8436 |pmc=5182420 |pmid=27994129}}</ref>
